    "result_text": "Green. Here's how I addressed the review.\n\n## Agreed and fixed: SHA-256-to-hex duplication\n\nI consolidated the digest-to-hex primitive into `IdentityKeyUtils` so it now exists once:\n\n- **`IdentityKeyUtils`** keeps `sha256Hex` (lower-case) as the sole digest routine, and adds `sha256UpperHex`, which just upper-cases that result \u2014 no second loop.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`**: deleted the private `sha256UpperHex` method; `customerCodeFor` now calls `IdentityKeyUtils.sha256UpperHex(...)`. Removed the now-unused `MessageDigest`, `NoSuchAlgorithmException`, and `StandardCharsets` imports.\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl.householdIdFor`**: replaced its inline digest loop with `IdentityKeyUtils.sha256UpperHex(key).substring(0, 12)` (behaviour identical \u2014 first 12 upper-case hex chars). Removed its now-unused hashing imports (kept `Locale`, still used by `normalizeForHousehold`).\n\nBehaviour is unchanged: `householdId` is still the first 12 upper-case hex chars, and the customer-code hash is still 8 upper-case hex chars.\n\n## Minor (optional) finding: left as-is\n\nThe double `soundex` computation per candidate in `findPossibleDuplicate` \u2014 I agree it's non-blocking and chose not to add a pre-filter. Introducing one would either duplicate the soundex-then-compare logic or complicate the stream for a micro-optimization on an in-memory list that's already iterated elsewhere in the create path (namesakes, city count, household size). It's the kind of change worth making only if profiling shows it matters, as the finding itself notes. If you'd prefer, I can hoist `soundex(existing.getLastName())` into a cheap prefilter before the key comparison \u2014 say the word.\n\nTests: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
